CHAPTER XII — MODDING FRAMEWORK
Obsidian Protocol is built from the ground up for modding.
Every system — units, factions, abilities, AI personalities, maps, campaigns, VR missions, and experimental mechanics — is fully data‑driven and exposed through structured, designer‑friendly tools.

This chapter defines:

Faction creation

Unit creation

Ability scripting

AI personality scripting

Map editor rules

Data schema

Packaging mods

Modding is a first‑class feature of Obsidian Protocol.

12.1 Faction Creation
Modders can create entirely new factions with unique:

Units

Buildings

Tech trees

Command units

Experimental units

Visual identity

AI personalities

VR mission integration

Faction Definition File
Factions are defined in JSON:

Code
{
  "id": "faction_wastelanders",
  "name": "Wastelanders",
  "description": "A nomadic faction specializing in stealth and salvage.",
  "command_units": ["core_waste", "insight_scrap"],
  "tech_tree": "tech_waste",
  "unit_list": ["scout_buggy", "scrap_mech", "sand_drone"]
}
Faction Components
Faction Identity — lore, theme, strengths, weaknesses

Command Units — strategic backbone

Tech Tree — progression path

Unit Roster — air, ground, sea, command, experimental

Visual Style — materials, colors, silhouettes

Audio Style — VO, effects, ambience

Faction Balancing
Modders can:

Adjust resource costs

Tune unit stats

Modify tech tree pacing

Add or remove experimental units

